= Coffee (Qahwa, 咖啡)

_Scope: this entry treats *Coffea arabica* L. and *C. canephora* (robusta), Rubiaceae — the roasted seed and its brew, with the husk-drink *qishr* and the green bean treated as distinct drug-forms. Status is unusual for this book and worth stating precisely: coffee is *absent from the Chinese and Indian classical canons* — a 19th-century arrival in China, a 17th-century one in India — but it is *genuinely classical in Tibb*, entering the living Greco-Arabic tradition in the 15th–16th centuries and receiving substantial treatment by Dāwūd al-Anṭākī (d. 1599) and a dedicated juridical-medical monograph by al-Jazīrī (1587). It is therefore not a "reasoned-only" substance in the way açaí or guaraná are: it is a *late entrant to a tradition still writing*. Two things about it repay attention. First, the classical Tibb mizāj is *Cold and Dry* — the opposite of the modern Western intuition of coffee as a hot stimulant — and the cardamom in Arabic coffee is the classical *muṣliḥ* correction for exactly that, still in the cup five centuries later. Second, coffee is the substance in this book where *preparation method has the clearest measurable consequence*: whether the brew passes through a paper filter determines whether it raises LDL cholesterol._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / botanical:* *_Coffea arabica_ L.* — ~60–70% of world production; an allotetraploid of natural hybrid origin (*_C. canephora_* × *_C. eugenioides_*), native to the Ethiopian highlands; lower in caffeine (~1.2% of dry bean) and conventionally the finer cup. *_Coffea canephora_ Pierre ex A. Froehner* — *robusta*; lowland West and Central African origin, roughly twice the caffeine (~2.2%), hardier and more disease-resistant. *_C. liberica_* and others are minor. Family *Rubiaceae* — *and the family is not a stranger to this book*: it also contains *Cinchona* (quinine) and *Gardenia jasminoides*, whose fruit *梔子 (zhīzǐ)* is a classical Bencao drug. *The family is in the Chinese canon; the genus is not.*#footnote[Species delimitation, the allotetraploid hybrid origin of _C. arabica_, and the caffeine ranges: standard coffee-botany and agronomy references. Rubiaceae membership shared with _Cinchona_ and _Gardenia_ is taxonomic fact; the point that 梔子 is a classical Bencao drug while _Coffea_ is not is noted to locate the plant relative to the Chinese canon.]

*Arabic — and the etymology needs care.* *قَهْوَة (qahwa)* is the beverage; *بُنّ (bunn)* is the bean and the plant; *قِشْر (qishr)* is the husk, and the drink made from it. *The standard lexicographic account derives* qahwa *from an older Arabic word for* wine — classical *qahwa* is attested as a name for wine, conventionally linked to the root *ق-ه-ي (q-h-y)*, "to have no appetite," the drink being held to blunt hunger. The name was transferred to the new beverage, which is itself a telling choice given the juridical controversy that followed (below).

*The widely repeated derivation from* Kaffa*, the Ethiopian region, is a folk etymology and should be flagged as such.* It is attractive — Kaffa is in the plant's homeland — but the Arabic word is attested in the wine sense before the beverage existed, and most lexicographers treat the resemblance as coincidence or as later reinforcement. *This entry does not adopt it.*#footnote[_qahwa_ as an older Arabic word for wine, and the q-h-y "to lack appetite" association: Lane, _An Arabic-English Lexicon_ (s.v. قهوة); standard Arabic lexicography. The Kaffa derivation is widespread in popular and even some scholarly writing but is not supported by the word's attestation history — flagged as folk etymology per this book's standard, not asserted as false beyond the evidence.]

*Amharic / Ethiopian:* *ቡና (buna)* — the word and the *buna* ceremony, the roasting-grinding-brewing ritual performed in three servings (*abol*, *tona*, *baraka*). *Ethiopia is the plant's genetic home*, and the gradient there from *forest* coffee through *semi-forest* and *garden* to *plantation* coffee preserves wild genetic diversity found nowhere else — a fact of real consequence given the breeding problems discussed under Climate.

*Turkish and European:* Ottoman Turkish *kahve* → Italian *caffè* → French *café*, Dutch *koffie*, German *Kaffee*, English *coffee* (via Dutch and Italian, 17th c.). *المخا (al-Makhā)*, the Yemeni Red Sea port through which the trade ran, gives English *mocha* — originally a provenance, now a flavour-name for coffee with chocolate, which is a complete semantic drift worth noting.

*Chinese:* *咖啡 (kāfēi)* — a *phonetic transliteration* with the mouth radical 口 marking both characters as sound-borrowings, exactly the convention used for foreign words. *No Bencao entry*; coffee reached China in the late 19th century, with French missionaries establishing Yunnan plantations in the 1890s. See the CCM section.

*Japanese:* *コーヒー (kōhī)*, and in *ateji* (phonetic kanji) *珈琲* — characters chosen by the Edo scholar *Udagawa Yōan*, reportedly for the resemblance of a branch of coffee cherries to *珈* (a hairpin ornament) strung with *琲* (beaded strings). A phonetic borrowing that is also a small visual pun.

*Sanskrit:* *no classical name.* Coffee reached India around 1600 — the tradition credits *Bābā Budan*, who is said to have carried seven seeds from Mocha to the Chandragiri hills of Karnataka (c. 1670), where the range still bears his name. Modern Hindi *कॉफ़ी (kŏfī)*, Tamil *காபி (kāpi)*; and *कॉफी / kāphī* has a *modern* dravyaguna treatment (see the Ayurveda section), which is not the same as classical attestation.

*Portuguese:* *café*; *cafezinho*, the small strong sweetened cup that is the Brazilian social unit of hospitality (already in this book's glossary). Brazil's own coffee vocabulary — *fazenda*, *ouro verde* ("green gold"), *café com leite* — is treated under Climate.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_No classical entry — coffee is a late-19th-century arrival in China, 咖啡 is a phonetic coinage, and nothing in the Bencao tradition concerns it. The reading below is reasoned extension. It is worth noting what makes this extension unusually awkward rather than unusually easy: Chinese medicine already has a fully developed drug in this functional slot — *茶 (chá)*, tea, with a classical Bencao entry and a large literature (see `green-tea.typ`) — so coffee arrives not into an empty category but alongside an incumbent, and the natural classical move is to read it against tea rather than on its own._

*Property (by reasoned extension):* Warm (溫) — *and this diverges from tea*, which the tradition reads as cool to slightly cold (微寒). The divergence rests on coffee's roasting, its drying-stimulating effect, and its tendency to provoke heat signs (irritability, insomnia, dry mouth) where tea does not. *Flagged as reasoned*; modern Chinese popular sources vary, some assigning 平 (neutral).

*Flavor:* Bitter (苦) — dominant and defining — with a secondary sweet (甘) in the roasted aroma and an astringent (澀) edge.

*Channels (by extension):* Heart (心), Stomach (胃), Liver (肝).

*Actions (by extension):*
- *提神醒腦 (tí shén xǐng nǎo)* — refreshes the spirit and awakens the brain. The primary action, and the one the drug is actually used for; *醒腦* names the effect precisely.
- *行氣 / 開鬱 (xíng qì / kāi yù)* — moves qi and opens constraint: the subjective loosening and lift, read in the Chinese idiom.
- *消食 (xiāo shí)* — disperses food stagnation; the after-meal use, convergent with the Tibb digestive register and with the Ayurvedic *dīpana* reading.
- *利尿 (lì niào)* — promotes urination; a straightforward and uncontested effect.

*Cautions (by extension):* *陰虛火旺* (yin vacuity with flaring fire) and heat patterns — the warm, drying, stimulating profile aggravates; insomnia and palpitations; *肝陽上亢* (ascendant Liver yang) with headache and irritability. A patient for whom the tradition would prescribe chrysanthemum (see `chrysanthemum.typ`) is a patient for whom coffee is the wrong beverage.#footnote[Reasoned placement only; _Coffea_ has no Bencao entry and no classical 性味歸經. The reading is extension from the drug's observed effects into classical categories, deliberately set against the classical tea attributions for contrast — see `green-tea.typ`. Modern Chinese popular sources vary in assigning 溫 or 平 to coffee; the divergence is flagged rather than resolved.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_A middle case, tiered honestly. Coffee has *no classical attestation* — no Sanskrit name, nothing in Caraka, Suśruta, or the nighaṇṭus, the plant having reached India only around 1600. But it is *not* merely reasoned-by-this-book either: *modern dravyaguna literature treats it*, P. V. Sharma's _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_ carrying a *kāphī* entry, which `green-tea.typ` already cites as the closest cross-system reasoning template for a post-classical stimulant beverage. So the status is: post-classical, with a modern dravyaguna treatment — a step above pure extension, and well short of canonical._

*Rasa:* Tikta (तिक्त, bitter — dominant), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent).

*Guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp), Sūkṣma (सूक्ष्म, subtle — it penetrates and reaches the *srotas* and the mind quickly).

*Vīrya:* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, heating) — converging with the reasoned Chinese 溫 and *diverging sharply from the classical Tibb Cold-Dry attribution* discussed below. *This is the entry's principal cross-system divergence and it is not resolved here*: the Indian and reasoned-Chinese readings run on the drug's stimulating, drying, heat-provoking effects; the Greco-Arabic reading runs on its bitterness and its blackness, both classical markers of *black bile* and therefore of cold-dry. Two coherent systems, one substance, opposite thermal placements.

*Vipāka:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).

*Prabhāva:* *None classically* — there is no classical entry to carry one. The candidate, if one is proposed, is *nidrā-nāśana* — the *abolition of sleep* — which is not predictable from tikta-kaṣāya rasa, laghu-rūkṣa guṇa, uṣṇa vīrya, and kaṭu vipāka: many bitter, light, dry, warm substances exist and almost none of them keeps a person awake. On the prabhāva criterion as Caraka states it (Sū. 26.67–72) this qualifies cleanly. *Flagged as modern interpretive*, applied by this book to a post-classical substance.

*Doṣa effect:* *Vāta-vardhaka* — *and this is the operative fact in modern Ayurvedic practice.* The laghu-rūkṣa-tīkṣṇa-sūkṣma combination is the canonical Vāta-aggravating profile, and the standard modern Ayurvedic objection to habitual coffee is precisely Vāta provocation: dryness, restlessness, irregular appetite and elimination, anxiety, broken sleep. *Pitta-vardhaka* via uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa (acidity, irritability). *Kapha-śāmaka* — the one direction in which it is useful, and the basis of its reasoned indication in heavy, dull, congested states.

*Karma:* _Nidrā-nāśana_ (sleep-abolishing), _medhya_ in the short term (alertness and attention — though habitual use is read as depleting rather than building), _dīpana_ and _pācana_ (the after-meal digestive use), _mūtrala_ (diuretic), _śrama-hara_ (fatigue-relieving, acutely), _rūkṣaṇa_ (drying). *Laṅghana* rather than *bṛṃhaṇa* in overall tendency — it reduces rather than builds, which is the dravyaguna statement of why it suits the heavy and burdens the depleted.#footnote[Post-classical status with a modern dravyaguna treatment: P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, _kāphī_ entry — cited in `green-tea.typ` as the template for post-classical stimulant beverages; verify the specific attributions against that edition. The Vāta-aggravation reading is the standard position in modern Ayurvedic clinical literature and converges with the modern pharmacological picture of anxiety, insomnia and GERD. Prabhāva criterion: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 26.67–72; the _nidrā-nāśana_ candidate is flagged as this book's interpretive proposal.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(no classical viruddha — post-classical substance; extensions of conditional rules plus a substantial modern tier; tags: S core saṃhitā, N nighaṇṭu, F folk/regional, M modern/pharmacological)_

- *(extended) Conditional:* _Doṣa-viruddha_ in Vāta excess and Vāta constitutions — the governing caution; _avasthā-viruddha_ on an empty stomach in the acid-prone, and in dehydration; _kāla-viruddha_ in the evening, and in autumn (the Vāta season) for Vāta types; _agni-viruddha_ is interesting in both directions, since coffee is *dīpana* acutely but burdens an irritated stomach.
- *(F) Folk/regional:* South Indian *filter coffee* is taken with substantial hot milk and sugar — a preparation that blunts the dryness and the bitterness, and which modern Ayurvedic practitioners often treat as the least objectionable form on exactly that reasoning. The Arabic cardamom pairing is the same corrective logic from another tradition (see Tibb).
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Caffeine pharmacology* applies in full as set out in `guarana.typ` and `green-tea.typ`: adenosine A₁/A₂A receptor antagonism, with secondary catecholamine effects.
  - *CYP1A2 — and the variation is large.* Caffeine is cleared principally by CYP1A2, whose activity varies severalfold between individuals by genotype and is *induced by smoking* and *markedly inhibited in pregnancy and by oral contraceptives*. Half-life runs ~3–6 hours in a typical adult but can exceed 10 hours in pregnancy. *This is why identical intakes produce quite different effects in different people*, and it is a better explanation of "coffee doesn't affect me" than tolerance alone.
  - *Drug interactions:* CYP1A2 substrate competition (fluvoxamine, ciprofloxacin markedly raise caffeine levels); additive stimulation with sympathomimetics; theophylline; lithium clearance; and reduced *non-haem iron absorption* when taken with meals — relevant in iron-deficiency, and avoidable by separating coffee from meals by an hour.
  - *Anxiety, insomnia, palpitations, GERD* — dose-dependent and the commonest practical limits.
  - *Dependence and withdrawal* — physical dependence is real; *caffeine withdrawal* (headache, fatigue, dysphoria, 12–24 h after cessation, lasting days) is a recognized clinical entity.
  - *Pregnancy* — limit total caffeine to *≤200 mg/day* per current obstetric guidance, consistent with the figure used across this book.
  - *The unfiltered-brew lipid effect* — see Modern Pharmacology; a *preparation-method* caution rather than a substance one, and the most actionable item in this entry.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

*This is coffee's classical tradition, and the only one it has.* Coffee enters Arabic medicine *late but genuinely* — too late for Avicenna (d. 1037) or Ibn al-Bayṭār (d. 1248), but squarely within the still-productive Tibb literature of the 15th–16th centuries. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ` for the humoral frame.

*The Sufi origin — the beginning of coffee drinking as such.* Coffee's use as a *beverage* begins in 15th-century *Yemen*, in Sufi circles, as a *devotional stimulant*: it was drunk to sustain wakefulness through night-long *dhikr* (remembrance) and vigil. The transmission is conventionally associated with the Yemeni Sufi *ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Shādhilī* and with *Muḥammad al-Dhabhānī* (d. c. 1470). *The plant was Ethiopian; the drink is Yemeni and Sufi*, and the earliest institution of coffee-drinking is religious rather than social or medical.

*The juridical controversy, which belongs in a materia medica because it was argued partly in medical terms.* Coffee's spread provoked a serious legal dispute: was *qahwa* an intoxicant (*khamr*), and therefore forbidden? The question was sharpened by the name itself, borrowed from a word for wine. In *1511* the governor of Mecca, *Khāʾir Beg*, banned it; the ban was overturned. Ottoman prohibitions followed and lapsed. The debate generated a dedicated literature, of which the fullest surviving example is *ʿUmdat al-ṣafwa fī ḥill al-qahwa* ("The Purity of Argument in Favour of the Lawfulness of Coffee") by *ʿAbd al-Qādir al-Jazīrī* (1587) — simultaneously a legal brief, a history, and a source for the medical opinions of the period. *Coffee is the one substance in this book whose permissibility was formally litigated.*

*Names:* *بُنّ (bunn)* the bean and plant; *قَهْوَة (qahwa)* the beverage; *قِشْر (qishr)* the husk and its drink. *Disambiguation:* *qishr* simply means "husk, peel," so the word appears elsewhere in this book in other compounds — *قشر الجوز (qishr al-jawz)*, walnut hull (see `black-walnut.typ`). Yemeni *qishr* is coffee-husk, not walnut.

*Mizāj:* *Cold and Dry* — commonly in the *1st* degree, with sources differing and some assigning Hot. *The Cold-Dry attribution is the standard one and is the single most counterintuitive fact in this entry*, since the modern Western intuition reads coffee as hot and stimulating. The classical reasoning is coherent: it is intensely *bitter* and *black*, and both are the markers of *sawdāʾ* (black bile), which is cold and dry; its drying effect on the body is observable; and its stimulation was understood as a *sharpening* rather than a heating. *Flagged for verification* against a specific al-Anṭākī edition, and flagged as genuinely variable across sources.

*Afʿāl (actions):* *Mujaffif* (desiccant — the governing action); *mufattiḥ* (deobstruent, opening obstructions of the liver and viscera); *hāḍim* and *muqawwī al-maʿida* (digestive, stomach-strengthening — the after-meal use); *mudirr li-l-bawl* (diuretic); *munabbih* / anti-soporific — the wakefulness action, which is what it was adopted for; *muqawwī al-dimāgh* in the alertness sense.

*Istemālāt (indications):* *amrāḍ al-balgham* (phlegmatic conditions — a Cold-Dry drying agent against cold-moist disease is humorally coherent); sluggish digestion and obstruction; headache of the heavy, phlegmatic type; lethargy and somnolence; and the devotional use above.

*Mawāneʿ / Iḥtirāzāt (contraindications):* *sawdāwī* (melancholic) temperaments and *amrāḍ al-sawdāʾ* — *because a Cold-Dry substance increases black bile*. This is the classical objection to habitual coffee, and it is worth pausing on: *the tradition's warning that coffee breeds melancholy, anxiety and sleeplessness in the susceptible is, in humoral dress, close to the modern caution about anxiety and insomnia and to the Ayurvedic Vāta objection* — three systems describing the same clinical population by three different mechanisms.

*Muṣliḥ (corrective) — and it is still in the cup.* The classical correction for a Cold-Dry drug is a *warming aromatic*, and Arabic coffee practice applies it exactly: *هَال (hāl,* cardamom) above all, with *cinnamon*, *cloves*, *ginger* and *saffron* regionally, and sugar. *Gulf qahwa with cardamom is not a flavouring preference but a five-century-old humoral correction that has outlived the theory that produced it* — one of the clearest surviving instances in this book of a *muṣliḥ* rule persisting as ordinary culinary practice. *Badal:* no formal substitute table entry located; the wakefulness register would be served by *qāt* in Yemen, a quite different drug.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *none, and none possible* — coffee postdates the Prophet by some eight centuries, and the tradition itself *debated whether it was lawful at all* (above). Any claim of Prophetic provenance for coffee is anachronistic on its face.#footnote[Dāwūd al-Anṭākī (d. 1599), _Tadhkirat ūlī al-albāb_ — the principal classical Tibb treatment of بن/قهوة; ʿAbd al-Qādir al-Jazīrī, _ʿUmdat al-ṣafwa fī ḥill al-qahwa_ (1587) — the dedicated juridical-and-historical monograph, and the standard primary source for the controversy, the 1511 Mecca ban under Khāʾir Beg, and the Sufi origin. The Cold-Dry mizāj is the common attribution with real variation across sources and is flagged for verification against a specific edition. The Sufi transmission via al-Shādhilī and al-Dhabhānī is the conventional account in the historical literature; the hagiographical elements should be treated as such.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

*No classical formula or preparation* — coffee postdates the Bencao tradition's reception of foreign drugs. Modern Chinese consumption is a 20th–21st-century phenomenon; Yunnan has grown arabica commercially since French missionary plantings in the 1890s and is now a significant producer. Where modern Chinese dietetics discusses 咖啡 it does so in the reasoned terms set out above, against the classical 茶 register.

=== Ayurveda

*No classical formula.* The living Indian tradition is *South Indian filter coffee* — coarse-ground coffee (often with 10–30% chicory) brewed by slow gravity percolation in a two-chamber steel *filter*, mixed with boiled milk and sugar, and pulled between two vessels (*davara* and tumbler) to froth and cool it. Descended from the Bābā Budan introduction and the later colonial plantations of the Western Ghats. Modern dravyaguna treats *kāphī* as above.

=== Western / Greco-Roman

*No Greco-Roman attestation whatever* — coffee was unknown to Dioscorides, Galen and Pliny, and no classical Mediterranean text describes it. *The European reception is early-modern, and began as botany and medicine rather than as refreshment:*
- *Leonhard Rauwolf* (1582) gives the first European printed description of the beverage, from his travels in Aleppo.
- *Prospero Alpini*, *De Plantis Aegypti* (1592), gives the *first European botanical description of the plant*, observed in Cairo — the standard citation for coffee's entry into European natural history.
- *Coffeehouses* spread from Venice (1640s) and Oxford/London (1650s); the London houses became the "penny universities," and *Lloyd's of London* began as Edward Lloyd's coffeehouse. The *Women's Petition Against Coffee* (1674) is a real pamphlet and a useful corrective to any assumption that the drink was uncontroversial in Europe either.
- *A flagged legend:* the story that *Pope Clement VIII* tasted coffee and "baptized" it, overruling advisers who called it a Muslim drink, is *apocryphal* — it has no contemporary documentation and first appears much later. Charming, repeated everywhere, and not evidence.
- *Medical reception:* coffee entered European pharmacy as a drug for headache, lethargy and digestive sluggishness before it settled into daily use — and 17th–18th c. European physicians, working in the inherited humoral idiom, inherited the Arabic debate about its qualities along with the substance.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic)

The constitutive preparations, all still current:
- *قهوة (qahwa)* — in the Gulf and Levant, lightly roasted, coarsely ground, boiled in a *dallah*, and *spiced with cardamom* (the muṣliḥ, above), sometimes saffron, clove or cinnamon; served unsweetened in small handleless cups with dates.
- *Turkish/Ottoman method* — very fine grind boiled with sugar in an *ibrik/cezve*, grounds served in the cup; the method that carried coffee into Europe.
- *قشر (qishr)* — the *Yemeni husk drink*: the dried coffee *cherry husk* brewed with ginger and cinnamon. *Historically this was the commoner domestic preparation in Yemen*, the bean being the export; it is a genuinely distinct drug-form, lower in caffeine and quite different in character, and the modern specialty-trade "cascara" is the same material rediscovered.
- *Ethiopian buna ceremony* — included here as the plant's home tradition rather than a Tibb one: green beans roasted, ground and brewed in a *jebena* before the guests, in three successive servings of decreasing strength.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

*Coffee is the most climate-constrained substance in this book*, and the constraint is tightening. Arabica requires a narrow band — roughly *18–22 °C* mean temperature, *1,200–2,000 m* in the tropics, reliable rainfall with a dry period to trigger flowering, and no frost. Robusta tolerates hotter, wetter lowlands. The resulting *"coffee belt"* runs roughly 25° N to 30° S, and almost every producing region sits inside it on borrowed margins. See `climates.typ`.

=== Ethiopian highland (the origin, and the gene bank)

Köppen Cwb at 1,500–2,200 m. The plant's *centre of origin and of genetic diversity*, with a continuum from *forest coffee* (wild, under canopy) through *semi-forest* and *garden* to *plantation*. *The wild populations matter beyond sentiment*: cultivated arabica worldwide descends from a very narrow founder base and is correspondingly vulnerable, and Ethiopian forest material is the principal reservoir of resistance traits — which places the crop's long-term security inside forests that are themselves under pressure.

=== Yemeni terraces (the first cultivation)

Hot-arid mountains, terraced and irrigated at 1,000–2,000 m; the first deliberate cultivation and the origin of the trade through *al-Makhā*. *Cares:* the Yemeni register retains *qishr* as the household drink and exports the bean — a division of the crop between domestic and trade use that is itself a climatic-economic adaptation.

=== Latin American highland (the production heartland, and Brazil)

Köppen Cwa/Cfa/Aw at altitude: Brazil (Minas Gerais, São Paulo, Espírito Santo), Colombia, Central America. *Brazil has been the world's largest producer since the 1840s*, and the crop shaped the country: the *fazenda* plantation system built on enslaved labour until 1888 and then on Italian and Japanese immigrant labour; *ouro verde*, "green gold"; and the *República do café com leite* (1894–1930), in which the presidency alternated between the coffee oligarchy of São Paulo and the dairy interests of Minas Gerais — *a national political arrangement named after a drink*. The 1930s Depression saw the state burn surplus coffee to support prices. *Climatic vulnerability is specific and recurrent*: the southern Brazilian producing zone sits near the frost margin, and events such as the 1975 "Black Frost" have destroyed large fractions of the crop and moved world prices.

=== Southeast Asian lowland (robusta)

Vietnam is the world's second producer and overwhelmingly *robusta*, grown hot and low; Indonesia likewise, with its own fermented and aged specialities. The arabica/robusta split is at bottom a *climatic* division of labour, and the global warming trend favours the robusta end of it.

=== Disease, and a precedent already in this book

*Coffee leaf rust (_Hemileia vastatrix_)* destroyed the coffee industry of Ceylon in the 1860s–80s — and the island replanted with *tea*, which is why Sri Lanka is a tea country today. `red-tea.typ` records the same event from the tea side. *A fungal pathogen rewrote the agricultural identity of a country*, and the rust remains a live threat, with major Latin American outbreaks in the 2010s.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_ / _kāla-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

Three transpositions. First and most consequential, *the crop itself may not be transposable for much longer*: projections of suitable arabica land under warming are severe, and the adaptive responses — moving upslope, shifting to robusta, breeding from the Ethiopian reservoir — are each constrained. Second, *dose and context*: a drink taken in small spiced cups after meals, or in a Sufi vigil, has become a large-volume all-day beverage, which is a _mātrā_- and _kāla_-order change the classical register never contemplated, and it is in that register that the anxiety-insomnia-reflux complaints arise. Third, *the correctives were dropped*: the cardamom, the dates, the milk, the small cup were not decoration but the tradition's own moderation of a drug it classified as Cold-Dry and habit-forming. See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Brewed coffee is essentially non-nutritive — a few kcal per cup, trace minerals, some niacin — so the profile below is pharmacological rather than nutritional.

*Key constituents:*
- *Caffeine* — ~*80–100 mg per standard brewed cup* (consistent with the figures used in `green-tea.typ` and `red-tea.typ`), with wide variation by bean, dose and method; espresso is higher *per volume* and usually lower *per serving*. Robusta roughly doubles arabica.
- *Chlorogenic acids (CGAs)* — the major polyphenols, abundant in the *green* bean and *substantially degraded by roasting*; the principal reason green-coffee extract and brewed coffee are different products with different claims.
- *Cafestol and kahweol* — *diterpenes, and the most practically important constituents in this entry*. See below.
- *Trigonelline* — degraded on roasting partly to *niacin*, so darker roasts deliver modestly more; also a precursor of aroma compounds.
- *Melanoidins* — brown roast polymers, antioxidant-active; *acrylamide*, formed in roasting (see below); *N*-methylpyridinium.

*The filtration effect — the clearest preparation-dependent pharmacology in this book.* *Cafestol* (and kahweol) *raise serum LDL cholesterol*, measurably and dose-dependently, by interfering with cholesterol homeostasis. They are *retained by paper filters*. Consequently:
- *Paper-filtered coffee* (drip, pour-over, filter machines) — negligible diterpene content, *no meaningful LDL effect*.
- *Unfiltered coffee* (French press/cafetière, Turkish/Greek, Scandinavian boiled, and to a lesser extent espresso and moka) — retains diterpenes and *does raise LDL* with regular consumption.
*A person told to lower their cholesterol who drinks several French-press coffees daily has an actionable, specific, and almost universally overlooked lever — and it is a change of method, not of quantity.* Metal-mesh and cloth filters retain far less than paper.

*Clinical and epidemiological:*
- *Mortality and cardiometabolic* — large prospective cohorts associate moderate consumption (~3–4 cups/day) with *lower all-cause mortality* and lower type 2 diabetes incidence. *Observational*, with the standing confounding caveats (smoking, socioeconomic patterning, reverse causation in the unwell); the consistency across cohorts is notable but does not establish causation.
- *Liver* — the most consistent signal in the literature: inverse associations with *cirrhosis, liver fibrosis progression, and hepatocellular carcinoma*, with dose-response and plausible mechanisms. Stronger and more consistent than the cardiovascular associations.
- *Neurological* — inverse association with *Parkinson's disease*, robust across cohorts; weaker and less consistent signals for Alzheimer's.
- *Blood pressure* — acute pressor effect, with substantial tolerance on habitual use; habitual coffee is not an important driver of hypertension in most people, but acute effects matter for measurement and in the unaccustomed.
- *CYP1A2 and myocardial infarction* — studies suggest *slow metabolizers* may have increased MI risk at high intake while fast metabolizers do not. *Preliminary and not yet actionable*, but it is the most interesting pharmacogenetic thread here.
- *Acrylamide* — present as a roasting product; a theoretical carcinogenic concern at dietary exposures that remains unresolved, and it should be weighed against the generally favourable epidemiology rather than in isolation.
- *Bone and iron* — modest calcium loss at high intakes, and *inhibition of non-haem iron absorption* when taken with meals, which is the practically relevant one in iron deficiency.

*Safe daily intake / upper limit:*
- *Healthy non-pregnant adults:* up to *~400 mg caffeine/day* (roughly 4 standard cups), per current EFSA and FDA guidance and consistent with the figure used in `guarana.typ`.
- *Pregnancy and lactation:* *≤200 mg/day*.
- *Adolescents and children:* lower; routine coffee is not appropriate for children.
- *Avoid or restrict:* anxiety disorders and panic disorder, insomnia, cardiac arrhythmias, uncontrolled hypertension, significant GERD; and with CYP1A2-inhibiting drugs (fluvoxamine, ciprofloxacin).
- *If LDL cholesterol is the concern:* use a *paper filter* — the single most specific recommendation this entry can make.
- *Withdrawal* is real; taper rather than stop abruptly if headache matters.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Caffeine content, adenosine mechanism and intake ceilings: EFSA and FDA guidance (~400 mg/day adults, ≤200 mg/day pregnancy), consistent with `guarana.typ` and `green-tea.typ`. CYP1A2 as the principal clearance route, its severalfold interindividual variation, induction by smoking and marked inhibition in pregnancy: standard clinical pharmacology. Cafestol and kahweol as LDL-raising diterpenes retained by paper filtration: a well-replicated body of controlled feeding and trial work from the 1980s onward — among the better-established facts in nutrition and among the least widely known. Cohort associations for mortality, type 2 diabetes, liver disease and Parkinson's: large prospective cohorts and meta-analyses — observational, with confounding caveats stated; the hepatic signal is the most consistent. CYP1A2 genotype and myocardial infarction: Cornelis et al. and subsequent work — preliminary. Acrylamide: unresolved at dietary exposure levels. All modern figures require current verification.]

