- ʿAbd al-Qādir al-Jazīrī, _ʿUmdat al-ṣafwa fī ḥill al-qahwa_ (1587) — the dedicated monograph on coffee's lawfulness, and the principal primary source for the Sufi origin, the 1511 Mecca ban, and the medical opinions of the period.
- Dāwūd al-Anṭākī, _Tadhkirat ūlī al-albāb_ (d. 1599) — the classical Tibb treatment of بن / قهوة; mizāj attribution flagged for verification against a specific edition.
- Lane, _An Arabic-English Lexicon_ (قهوة, بن, قشر) — the wine-word etymology and the Kaffa folk-etymology question.
- Leonhard Rauwolf (1582), first European printed description; Prospero Alpini, _De Plantis Aegypti_ (1592), first European botanical description.
- P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_ — the modern _kāphī_ entry (cited also in `green-tea.typ`); Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 26.67–72 for the prabhāva criterion.
- Coffee-botany and agronomy literature on _C. arabica_ allotetraploid origin, the arabica/robusta climatic division, and Ethiopian forest-coffee genetic diversity; climate-projection literature on arabica suitability.
- Coffee-history literature on the Sufi transmission, the Ottoman and European receptions, the Bābā Budan introduction to Karnataka, Brazilian plantation history and the _república do café com leite_; the _Hemileia vastatrix_ Ceylon collapse (see `red-tea.typ`).
- EFSA and FDA caffeine guidance; clinical pharmacology references on CYP1A2; the controlled-trial literature on cafestol/kahweol and serum lipids; prospective cohorts and meta-analyses on coffee and mortality, type 2 diabetes, liver disease and Parkinson's — all verify against current literature.
